Direct Collocation Methods
Direct collocation methods are a family of numerical techniques for solving optimal control problems by discretizing both the control and state trajectories at predefined time points, known as collocation points. Instead of directly solving the continuous-time dynamics and optimization simultaneously, these methods convert the problem into a large but finite-dimensional nonlinear programming (NLP) problem. This transformation makes it easier to incorporate complex nonlinear constraints (e.g., bounds on the state or control variables, path constraints) and nonlinear cost functions directly into the optimization problem. In direct collocation:

